<reference_puzzle index="1" source="eval" puzzle_id="a47bf94d" lines="178-189">
<description>
Transmit colored tokens along wire networks, converting each to its complementary 3×3 pattern.

Input structure:
- bg = background. Non-bg cells form wire networks and 3×3 tokens (solid blocks or X/diagonal patterns) near wire endpoints.

Rule:
- Every solid 3×3 token is replaced in-place by a diamond (center + 4 cardinal neighbors).
- Each token near a wire endpoint sends its complement to the opposite endpoint: solid emits X, X emits diamond.

Key insight: Token shape cycles solid → X → diamond; wires route each complement to the far end.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="2" source="train" puzzle_id="13f06aa5" lines="36-51">
<description>
Extend each colored marker from its directional sprite to a grid edge, painting a dotted ray and the whole edge side.

Input structure:
- The background is the dominant color.
- One repeated body color forms small 8-connected directional sprites.
- Each marker is a singleton non-background, non-body color embedded in one sprite.

Rule:
- The body pixels around a marker lie mostly behind it; the opposite direction is the marker's ray direction.
- Paint every second pixel from the marker along that ray, then paint the entire boundary side reached by the ray.
- If two painted boundary sides meet at a corner, make that shared corner black.

Key insight:
- The same seven-pixel body sprite rotates to encode the direction; its local center-of-mass bias points away from the ray.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="3" source="train" puzzle_id="15660dd6" lines="33-54">
<description>
Combine each column's repeated template mask with its single solid color swatch.

Input structure:
- The grid is a table of framed panels separated by background rows/columns.
- The leftmost column gives one solid row-label color for each panel row.
- In each panel column, exactly one panel has a solid non-background interior,
  while the other panels share the same mask drawn in one template color on
  the background.

Rule:
- For every panel column, find the row containing the solid interior swatch.
- Rebuild one output panel using that row's left label as the frame color.
- Fill the repeated template mask with the swatch color and leave the mask
  background cells as background; separate rebuilt panels with one
  background column.

Key insight:
The table encodes a colorized stencil per column: the solid panel supplies
the paint color and frame label, while the duplicate template panels supply
the shape mask.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="4" source="train" puzzle_id="f0f8a26d" lines="30-44">
<description>
Rotate every foreground bar 90 degrees around its own center on the same canvas.

Input structure
The background is the dominant color, and all foreground pixels share one color.
Each 4-connected foreground object is a solid one-cell-thick horizontal or vertical
bar with odd length, with single-pixel bars allowed.

Rule
Clear the canvas to the background, then redraw every bar with its midpoint, length,
and color preserved while swapping horizontal and vertical orientation.

Key insight
The bars are independent objects: their centers and lengths identify the output
placement, so no matching, ordering, or global symmetry is needed.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="5" source="eval" puzzle_id="16b78196" lines="6-20">
<description>
Assemble colored sprites into a central bar's notches, chaining successive pieces along fixed-width strips.

Input structure:
- bg = most common color. Largest monochrome component is a solid "bar" with boundary notches (bg holes opening to its bbox edge).
- Several smaller colored sprites scattered around the bar.

Rule:
- Each bar notch is filled by a root piece whose shape exactly covers the hole.
- The root defines a strip (column-span or row-span) for its chain.
- Successive pieces fill the bg cells inside the previous piece's bbox, locked to the same strip width.
- Multiple chains grow independently from different bar notches.

Key insight: The root piece's extent perpendicular to the chain direction locks the strip width for all subsequent pieces in that chain.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="6" source="train" puzzle_id="3ad05f52" lines="128-142">
<description>
Paint the maze corridor and framed rooms lying on shortest paths between the two colored endpoint components.

Input structure
- The grid has a dominant background, one wall color forming thin orthogonal frames, and one endpoint/fill color.
- The endpoint/fill color forms exactly two 4-connected solid rectangular components; their larger side lengths define the framed room sizes.

Rule
- Treat wall pixels as blocked and every other pixel as passable.
- Find every passable pixel that lies on a shortest 4-connected path between the two endpoint components.
- Also fill any framed square room of an endpoint-derived size that intersects that shortest-path mask.

Key insight
- Shortest paths identify the route through open connector corridors, while the wall-bordered square rooms need to be expanded from the route centerline to their full interior.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="7" source="train" puzzle_id="e729b7be" lines="8-20">
<description>
Complete the scene by adding the point-reflected copy of the colored object across the central marker.

Input structure
- The grid has odd dimensions, a dominant background, and a center-row marker color appearing only at the left edge, center, and right edge.
- A single divider color fills the center column except for the center marker.
- All remaining foreground pixels form the object to copy, entirely on the left side of the divider.
Rule
- Keep the input unchanged.
- For every object pixel, paint the pixel rotated 180 degrees around the center marker with the same color.
Key insight
- The center marker is the rotation center; the yellow-looking divider is not mirrored, it only separates the source from the empty target side.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="8" source="train" puzzle_id="b745798f" lines="18-33">
<description>
Expand each 3-pixel L marker into a same-color corner L on an empty background.

Input structure
- The grid is an odd square with one dominant background color.
- Every foreground object is a monochromatic 4-connected 3-pixel L inside a
  2x2 box.

Rule
- Clear the grid to the background.
- For each marker, map its L orientation to the corresponding outer corner
  and draw that corner L in the marker color with arm length half the grid.

Key insight
- The small marker is a miniature corner; its orientation, not its position,
  chooses which outer corner receives the expanded L.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="9" source="train" puzzle_id="aa62e3f4" lines="18-35">
<description>
Replace the outlined object with the outside contour of that outline in the marker color.

Input structure:
- A dominant background surrounds one 8-connected foreground outline.
- The outline is the unique largest foreground color.
- One or more smaller interior colors are inside the outline; the marker color
  is the unique smallest non-outline color.

Rule:
- Erase all original foreground pixels to background.
- Paint every background pixel that is 4-adjacent to the outline with the
  marker color.
- Keep the grid shape unchanged.

Key insight: The colored interior marker supplies the paint color, while the
largest outline supplies only the contour whose outside edge is traced.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="10" source="train" puzzle_id="17829a00" lines="21-36">
<description>
Move each colored object vertically until it touches the matching top or bottom border bar.

Input structure:
- The background is the dominant color.
- The top row is a full-width bar in one foreground color and the bottom row is a full-width bar in another.
- All other foreground pixels are 8-connected monochrome objects whose color matches one of those bars.

Rule:
- Top-bar-colored objects move upward so their bbox top is row 1.
- Bottom-bar-colored objects move downward so their bbox bottom is the row above the bottom bar.
- A downward-moving one-column segment whose landing overlaps its source becomes the continuous vertical segment swept between them.

Key insight:
The horizontal bars are destination anchors: color determines which border an object slides toward, while columns and object shape are otherwise preserved.
